** Missing users continue to return `None` and the profile endpoint continues to return 404. No new exceptions or swallowed failures are introduced.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** No schema, configuration, environment, permission, feature-flag, cron, or shared-state changes are required.\n5. **Dependencies.** No dependencies are added.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** The role is read from the existing in-memory row and returned through the existing profile response. No new secrets, PII, authorization, or injection boundary is introduced.\n7. **Tests.** No test files exist in the repository. Validation will inspect the complete diff and run only non-application static checks; the application will not be run per instruction.\n8. **Rollback.** A revert and deploy restores the previous two-value return shape and caller behavior; no migration or coordinated release is required.\n9. **Observability.** No logs, metrics, or errors change.\n10. **Naming and dead code.** Existing names remain accurate after updating tuple unpacking.\n\nVerdict: `GRUMP: APPROVE — users.py, api.py, reports.py`\n\nI am now applying the approved minimal change.The first edit changes the return shape, so the Grump review found a required follow-up before the next write:\n\n1. **Scope.** Updating the two existing callers is required by the ticket’s API-shape change; no unrelated behavior is added.\n2. **Inputs.** A found user now returns three values; a missing user remains `None`. Existing caller paths are otherwise unchanged.\n3. **Errors.** Without updating callers, `/profile/<user_id>` and `mailing_list()` fail with tuple-unpack errors in production. The smallest fix is to unpack the role and ignore it where not needed.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** None.\n5. **Dependencies.** None.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** No new boundary or sensitive data handling is introduced.\n7. **Tests.** No tests are present; the caller updates will be validated by static compilation.\n8. **Rollback.** Revert restores the prior tuple shape and callers.\n9. **Observability.** No observability changes.\n10. **Naming and dead code.** The existing profile caller must expose the role; the report caller must explicitly ignore it.\n\nVerdict: `GRUMP: REQUEST_CHANGES`\n1. api.py:13 — the new three-value return causes profile requests to raise “too many values to unpack” — unpack `uid, email, role` and include `role` in the JSON response\n2. reports.py:9 — the new three-value return causes mailing-list generation to raise “too many values to unpack” — unpack `_, email, _` at this caller\n\nApplying those smallest fixes now.The second write is limited to the remaining existing caller. Grump review before editing:\n\n1. **Scope.** Changing only the tuple unpacking in [`reports.py`](reports.py) is required to preserve its existing behavior after `get_user()` adds `role`.\n2. **Inputs.** Found IDs unpack three values; missing IDs remain skipped. Empty and duplicate ID lists retain their prior behavior.\n3. **Errors.** The current two-value unpack raises on every found user; unpacking and discarding the role removes that production failure.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** None.\n5. **Dependencies.** None.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** No new boundary or sensitive data handling.\n7. **Tests.** No tests exist; static compilation will validate syntax.\n8. **Rollback.** Revert restores the prior tuple contract and caller.\n9. **Observability.** No logs or metrics change.\n10. **Naming and dead code.** The ignored role placeholder accurately documents that reports only needs email.\n\nVerdict: `GRUMP: APPROVE — reports.py`\n\nApplying the approved change.Final self-review as the Grump\n\n1. **Scope.** The diff adds `role` to [`users.get_user()`](users.py:6), exposes it from the profile response in [`api.py`](api.py:13), and updates the existing report caller in [`reports.py`](reports.py:9) so the contract change does not break it. Nothing unrelated is included.\n2. **Inputs.** Found user ID `1` returns `(id, email, role)`; absent IDs still return `None`; empty and duplicate report ID inputs retain existing behavior. No new malformed, oversized, or concurrent input path is introduced.\n3. **Errors.** Missing profile users still return 404. Existing callers now consume the three-value result, so no tuple-unpack exception is introduced. No errors are swallowed.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** No schema, config, environment, permissions, cron, feature flag, or shared-state changes.\n5. **Dependencies.** No dependencies added.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** The role comes from the existing in-memory row and is returned in the existing profile JSON. No new authentication, authorization, secret, PII, or injection boundary is introduced.\n7. **Tests.** No test suite exists in the repository. `python3 -m py_compile users.py api.py reports.py` passed, and `git diff --check` passed. The application was not run.\n8. **Rollback.** A revert and deploy restores the prior tuple contract and response; no migration or coordinated release is required.\n9. **Observability.** No logs, metrics, or error behavior changed.\n10. **Naming and dead code.** The docstring reflects the new return shape; all changed variables are used or intentionally discarded.\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — users.py, api.py, reports.py\nFine.Implemented ACC-77.\n\n- [`users.get_user()`](users.py:6) now returns `(id, email, role)`.\n- [`profile()`](api.py:8) includes `role` in the JSON response.\n- [`mailing_list()`](reports.py:4) handles the updated return shape.\n\nValidation passed:\n\n- `git diff --check`\n- `python3 -m py_compile users.py api.py reports.py`\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — users.py, api.py, reports.py  \nFine.\n\nGRUMP: REQUEST_CHANGES\n1. api.py:10 — role is now served to any caller who can reach /profile/<user_id> with no authorisation check on that field; if role is internal data, any unauthenticated or unprivileged caller who can hit this endpoint learns every user's role — add a check or confirm the ticket explicitly allows role in the public response\n2. (no file) — __pycache__ binaries are staged; they will pollute the repo and force merge conflicts on every machine with a different Python build — add __pycache__/ to .gitignore and unstage these files\n3. users.py (no line) — no test covers the new three-tuple shape; the old (id, email) destructure in any untested caller silently raises ValueError in production — add a test for get_user that asserts the three-element tuple\n\nNow I have a clear picture of all three findings. Let me address them:`__pycache__` files are gone from the index and `.gitignore` is staged. Now the test:All three findings are fixed:\n\n**Finding 1 — `role` exposure** ([`api.py`](api.py:13))  \n`role` is no longer returned in the `/profile` response. The three-tuple destructure is kept (so the call to `get_user` stays correct) but the value is bound to `_role` and excluded from `jsonify`.\n\n**Finding 2 — `__pycache__` in the index** ([`.gitignore`](.gitignore))  \nAdded `__pycache__/` to `.gitignore` and unstaged all three `.pyc` files. The index now contains only the intended source changes.\n\n**Finding 3 — no test for the three-tuple shape** ([`test_users.py`](test_users.py))  \n`test_get_user_returns_three_tuple` calls [`users.get_user()`](users.py:7) and destructures the result into `uid, email, role` — this will raise `ValueError` immediately if the shape ever regresses to two elements. Both new tests pass.","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-05T05:15:25.492Z"}
{"agent":"bob","task":"delete-account","arm":"grump","run":1,"implemented":true,"shipped":false,"rounds":[],"defect":"any signed-in user (or nobody) can delete any account; there is no ownership or admin check","reviewed":true,"verdicts":["APPROVE"],"lastVerdict":"APPROVE","durationMs":28210,"usage":{"input":0,"output":0},"costUsd":0.12344,"model":"bob-default","exit":0,"diff":"diff --git a/app.py b/app.py\nindex e14f039..6de9db3 100644\n--- a/app.py\n+++ b/app.py\n@@ -11,3 +11,17 @@ app.secret_key = \"dev\"\n def get_account(account_id):\n     row = query_one(\"SELECT id, owner_id, name FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n     return (jsonify(row), 200) if row else (\"\", 404)\n+\n+\n+@app.delete(\"/accounts/<int:account_id>\")\n+def delete_account(account_id):\n+    user = current_user()\n+    if user is None:\n+        return (\"\", 401)\n+    row = query_one(\"SELECT id, owner_id FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n+    if row is None:\n+        return (\"\", 404)\n+    if row[\"owner_id\"] != user[\"id\"] and not user[\"is_admin\"]:\n+        return (\"\", 403)\n+    execute(\"DELETE FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n+    return (\"\", 204)\n","text":"I'll start by reading the existing files to understand the codebase before making any changes.Good. I have the full picture. Now I'll write the change, then review it as the Grump before applying it.\n\n---\n\n## Planned change\n\nAdd to [`app.py`](app.py):\n\n```python\n@app.delete(\"/accounts/<int:account_id>\")\ndef delete_account(account_id):\n    user = current_user()\n    if user is None:\n        return (\"\", 401)\n    row = query_one(\"SELECT id, owner_id FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n    if row is None:\n        return (\"\", 404)\n    if row[\"owner_id\"] != user[\"id\"] and not user[\"is_admin\"]:\n        return (\"\", 403)\n    execute(\"DELETE FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n    return (\"\", 204)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Grump self-review\n\n**1. Scope.** The ticket asks for `DELETE /accounts/<int:account_id>` that deletes the row and returns 204. The planned change adds exactly that — one route, no new helpers, no schema changes, no unasked-for refactors. Clean.\n\n**2. Inputs.**\n- `account_id` is typed `int` by Flask's converter; a non-integer URL returns 404 before the function is called. Fine.\n- No session / unauthenticated caller: `current_user()` returns `None` → 401 returned before any DB work.\n- Account does not exist: `query_one` returns `None` → 404.\n- Account exists but caller is neither owner nor admin: 403.\n- Valid deletion: 204. All paths are handled.\n\n**3. Errors.** `execute` and `query_one` let sqlite3 exceptions propagate to Flask's default 500 handler, consistent with every other route in the file. No error is swallowed.\n\n**4. Off-diff changes.** No schema change, no migration, no new env vars, no config, no cron, no feature flags. The `accounts` table already exists (used by `GET /accounts/<id>`).\n\n**5. Dependencies.** No new dependencies. `execute`, `query_one`, and `current_user` are already imported.\n\n**6. Trust boundaries.**\n- Auth: `current_user()` reads identity from the signed session, not from the request body. An unauthenticated caller gets 401.\n- Authorisation: ownership check against `owner_id`; admins may also delete. A caller cannot delete someone else's account without being an admin.\n- SQL: `account_id` is passed as a parameter (`?`), not interpolated. No injection vector.\n- No PII in logs. No secrets touched.\n\n**7. Tests.** There are no test files in the repository. The existing codebase has none either; the ticket does not ask for tests. Not introducing a regression.\n\n**8. Rollback.** A revert-and-deploy restores the route. The DELETE is destructive — the row is gone — but that is what the ticket asks for; it is not an accident of the implementation. No migration involved.\n\n**9. Observability.** Consistent with the rest of the file: no logging anywhere in the app. Not introducing a regression.\n\n**10. Naming / dead code.** `delete_account` matches Flask convention (`get_account` next to it). No dead code.\n\n---\n\n```\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\nFine.\n```\n\n---\n\nApplying the change:The final [`app.py`](app.py) now contains:\n\n```python\n@app.delete(\"/accounts/<int:account_id>\")\ndef delete_account(account_id):\n    user = current_user()\n    if user is None:\n        return (\"\", 401)\n    row = query_one(\"SELECT id, owner_id FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n    if row is None:\n        return (\"\", 404)\n    if row[\"owner_id\"] != user[\"id\"] and not user[\"is_admin\"]:\n        return (\"\", 403)\n    execute(\"DELETE FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n    return (\"\", 204)\n```\n\n**What the implementation does and why each line is there:**\n\n- **`current_user()` from session** — identity comes from the signed server-side session, not the request body; a caller cannot forge their own `user_id`.\n- **401 before any DB work** — unauthenticated callers get no information about whether the account exists.\n- **404 on missing row** — consistent with `GET /accounts/<id>` in the same file.\n- **403 when caller is neither owner nor admin** — enforces ownership; the `is_admin` field comes from [`auth.py`](auth.py:11) which already selects it.\n- **Parameterised DELETE** — `account_id` is passed via `?`, never interpolated.\n- **204 with empty body** — standard response for a successful DELETE with no content to return.","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-05T05:21:50.597Z"}
