— accurate. ✓\n\nNo findings.\n\n```\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\n```\n\nFine.\n\nGRUMP: BLOCK\n1. app.py:13 — /orders/search is unauthenticated and returns customer_name for any caller on the network — customer_name is PII; add authentication and authorisation before this route ships\n\nLet me read the current state of the file first.Now let me check what authentication infrastructure already exists in the project.No existing auth infrastructure. I'll check git log to understand what change was made before the review.There is no pre-existing auth mechanism in the project. The review finding requires authentication and authorisation on the `/orders/search` route before it can ship. I'll add a simple API-key check — the key is read from the environment so it can be rotated without code changes, and the decorator is applied only to the route that exposes PII.Let me verify the final file looks correct end-to-end:Here is what was done and why each decision maps directly to the review finding.\n\n---\n\n**Finding addressed:** `app.py:13` — `/orders/search` unauthenticated, exposes `customer_name` PII.\n\n**Changes in [`app.py`](app.py):**\n\n1. **[`_API_KEY`](app.py:10)** — read from the `API_KEY` environment variable at startup. Using the environment keeps the secret out of source control and makes it rotatable without a code change.\n\n2. **[`require_api_key`](app.py:13)** — a decorator that enforces two checks:\n   - If `API_KEY` was never set in the environment the service returns `503` rather than silently allowing unauthenticated access. This prevents a misconfigured deployment from opening the PII endpoint.\n   - Every request must carry an `Authorization: Bearer <key>` header that matches the configured key; anything else gets `401 Unauthorised`.\n\n3. **[`@require_api_key`](app.py:34)** applied only to `search_orders` — the route identified in the finding. The `GET /orders/<id>` route is unchanged because the review did not flag it.\n\nGRUMP: BLOCK\n1. app.py:20 — `auth[len(\"Bearer \"):] != _API_KEY` uses plain string equality; CPython short-circuits on the first differing byte, making the secret recoverable by timing oracle — replace with `not hmac.compare_digest(auth[len(\"Bearer \"):], _API_KEY)`\n\nTwo changes made to [`app.py`](app.py):\n\n1. **Line 2** — added `import hmac`.\n2. **Line 21** — replaced `auth[len(\"Bearer \"):] != _API_KEY` with `not hmac.compare_digest(auth[len(\"Bearer \"):], _API_KEY)`.\n\n[`hmac.compare_digest()`](app.py:21) performs a constant-time comparison, so the execution time is the same regardless of where the strings first differ, eliminating the timing-oracle vulnerability.","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-05T06:29:43.670Z"}
