added to `load_config()` but not to `_SECRET_KEYS` would log in plaintext — but that is a future authoring error, not a defect in this diff.\n3. **Errors.** `log.info` with a pre-built dict cannot raise. No swallowed exceptions.\n4. **Off-diff.** No schema, env var, or config changes outside `app.py`.\n5. **Dependencies.** None added.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** `DATABASE_URL`, `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`, and `SMTP_PASSWORD` are replaced with `***REDACTED***` before the log call. No secret reaches the log line.\n7. **Tests.** No test suite in the repo.\n8. **Rollback.** Plain revert + deploy.\n9. **Observability.** `Effective configuration: {'LOG_LEVEL': 'INFO', 'PORT': 8080, 'REGION': 'eu-west-1', 'DATABASE_URL': '***REDACTED***', ...}` — on-call sees the shape of the config and non-sensitive values immediately. Message is self-explanatory.\n10. **Naming.** `_SECRET_KEYS` and `_safe_config` are clear; module-private by convention.\n\n```\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\n```\nFine.\n\nGRUMP: REQUEST_CHANGES\n1. app.py:10 — any config key not in _SECRET_KEYS logs its value in plaintext; a new secret added tomorrow leaks immediately — invert the guard: maintain a denylist of known-safe keys and redact everything else, or redact all values by default and explicitly allowlist non-sensitive ones\n2. app.py:10 — nested values (a config dict containing a sub-key for a password or token) pass through unredacted — apply the redaction recursively or flatten only scalar string values\n\nLet me read the relevant file first.Both findings are clear:\n\n1. **Finding 1** — allowlist (denylist of safe keys) instead of denylist (allowlist of secret keys): any unknown key logs in plaintext.\n2. **Finding 2** — nested dicts pass through unredacted.\n\nThe fix: redact all values by default, allowlist keys whose values are safe to log, and apply the redaction recursively so nested dicts are also walked.Both findings are resolved:\n\n**Finding 1 — inverted guard (allowlist instead of denylist)**  \n`_SEC